VITE_API_BASE_URL=http://localhost:8000
VITE_WS_URL=ws://localhost:8000/ws

# Authentication provider: "none" for email-based login, "google" for Google OAuth
VITE_AUTH_PROVIDER=none

# Required only when VITE_AUTH_PROVIDER=google
VITE_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=your-google-client-id.apps.googleusercontent.com

# Pipeline-level feature overrides — set to "true" or "false" to lock for all users.
# When set, the toggle in Settings is grayed out with a tooltip indicating pipeline control.
# Leave unset (or commented out) to let each user control the feature themselves.
#
# These features have a cascading "russian doll" dependency: AI ⊆ Transcription ⊆ In Review.
#   - Enabling AI forces Transcription on (AI needs the transcript to work).
#   - Enabling Transcription forces In Review on (transcription runs inside the In Review flow).
# A child forced on by its parent is shown enabled-and-locked in Settings, whether the
# parent was turned on via its UI toggle or via the override below. So, for example,
# forcing AI on effectively enables all three; forcing In Review off does NOT disable
# In Review while a parent (Transcription or AI) is on.
# VITE_FEATURE_TRANSCRIPTION=
# VITE_FEATURE_IN_REVIEW=
# VITE_FEATURE_AI=

# Follow Along stands outside that chain — it only moves the local selection.
# VITE_FEATURE_FOLLOW_ALONG=

# Follow Along: mirror a live review session's current clip.
#
# The broker and topic belong to whichever review player the site runs, so DNA
# ships with no defaults — Follow Along stays off until both are set. The
# session directory is optional; without it, a session name can be typed by hand.
#
# These two ARE player-agnostic: the announcement is parsed by
# createCurrentClipParser (@dna/core), which takes the element names carrying
# each field, so a player whose payload names things differently is configured
# rather than patched. The session directory below is not yet so — see there.
#
# Requires versions to carry an external id: set
# PRODTRACK_VERSION_EXTERNAL_REF_FIELD on the backend to the production
# tracking field holding the review player's clip id.
# VITE_FOLLOW_ALONG_BROKER_URL=ws://mq1.example.com:61614/stomp
# VITE_FOLLOW_ALONG_TOPIC=/topic/itview/current_clip.xml
#
# The session directory is read from the browser, and directories generally do
# not send CORS headers — so point this at a same-origin path and let the server
# in front of DNA map it, rather than at the directory's own host. nginx does
# this via REVIEW_SESSIONS_URL (frontend/default.conf.template); `npm run dev`
# reads the same variable (see vite.config.ts).
#
# KNOWN LIMITATION — the directory must currently be an edbot session broker.
# Unlike the broker and topic above, this is not a generic endpoint: DNA appends
# edbot's own path (/edbotproxy/rest/show/{show}/sessions) to REVIEW_SESSIONS_URL
# and reads edbot's JSON shape. Aiming REVIEW_SESSIONS_URL at a different
# directory therefore 404s — it is not a matter of configuration. A site running
# something else supplies its own fetcher returning ReviewSession[] (replacing
# fetchReviewSessions in @dna/core/followAlong); there is no config seam for it
# yet. Leave VITE_FOLLOW_ALONG_SESSIONS_URL unset in that case: the rest of
# Follow Along works without a directory.
#
# The directory also reports what each session member is watching, which is what
# lets DNA tell competing announcements on a shared topic apart. Without it,
# a session name can still be typed by hand, but nothing arbitrates between
# publishers announcing under the same name.
# VITE_FOLLOW_ALONG_SESSIONS_URL=/review-sessions

# Chrome extension ID from chrome://extensions (DNA Production Tracking Tab Sync)
# VITE_PRODTRACK_TAB_SYNC_EXTENSION_ID=

# Optional: link shown in the install prompt (Chrome Web Store or docs)
# VITE_PRODTRACK_TAB_SYNC_INSTALL_URL=https://github.com/AcademySoftwareFoundation/dna/blob/main/prodtrack-tab-sync-extension/README.md
